NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000001 "package Plack;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000002 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000003 "use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000004 "use warnings;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000005 "use 5.008_001;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000006 "our $VERSION = '1.0052';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000007 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000008 "1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000009 "__END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000010 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000011 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000012 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000013 "Plack - Perl Superglue for Web frameworks and Web Servers (PSGI toolkit)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000014 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000015 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000017 "Plack is a set of tools for using the PSGI stack. It contains"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000018 "middleware components, a reference server and utilities for Web"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000019 "application frameworks. Plack is like Ruby's Rack or Python's Paste"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000020 "for WSGI."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000021 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000022 "See L<PSGI> for the PSGI specification and L<PSGI::FAQ> to know what"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000023 "PSGI and Plack are and why we need them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000024 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000025 "=head1 MODULES AND UTILITIES"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000026 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000027 "=head2 Plack::Handler"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000028 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000029 "L<Plack::Handler> and its subclasses contains adapters for web"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000030 "servers. We have adapters for the built-in standalone web server"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000031 "L<HTTP::Server::PSGI>, L<CGI|Plack::Handler::CGI>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000032 "L<FCGI|Plack::Handler::FCGI>, L<Apache1|Plack::Handler::Apache1>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000033 "L<Apache2|Plack::Handler::Apache2> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000034 "L<HTTP::Server::Simple|Plack::Handler::HTTP::Server::Simple> included"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000035 "in the core Plack distribution."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000036 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000037 "There are also many HTTP server implementations on CPAN that have Plack"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000038 "handlers."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000039 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000040 "See L<Plack::Handler> when writing your own adapters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000041 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000042 "=head2 Plack::Loader"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000044 "L<Plack::Loader> is a loader to load one L<Plack::Handler> adapter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000045 "and run a PSGI application code reference with it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000047 "=head2 Plack::Util"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000049 "L<Plack::Util> contains a lot of utility functions for server"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000050 "implementors as well as middleware authors."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000051 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000052 "=head2 .psgi files"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000053 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000054 "A PSGI application is a code reference but it's not easy to pass code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000055 "reference via the command line or configuration files, so Plack uses a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000056 "convention that you need a file named C<app.psgi> or similar, which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000057 "would be loaded (via perl's core function C<do>) to return the PSGI"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000058 "application code reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000059 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000060 "  # Hello.psgi"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000061 "  my $app = sub {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000062 "      my $env = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000063 "      # ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000064 "      return [ $status, $headers, $body ];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000065 "  };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000066 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000067 "If you use a web framework, chances are that they provide a helper"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000068 "utility to automatically generate these C<.psgi> files for you, such"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000069 "as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000071 "  # MyApp.psgi"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000072 "  use MyApp;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000073 "  my $app = sub { MyApp->run_psgi(@_) };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000075 "It's important that the return value of C<.psgi> file is the code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000076 "reference. See C<eg/dot-psgi> directory for more examples of C<.psgi>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000077 "files."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000078 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 "=head2 plackup, Plack::Runner"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 "L<plackup> is a command line launcher to run PSGI applications from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 "command line using L<Plack::Loader> to load PSGI backends. It can be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 "used to run standalone servers and FastCGI daemon processes. Other"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 "server backends like Apache2 needs a separate configuration but"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 "C<.psgi> application file can still be the same."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 "If you want to write your own frontend that replaces, or adds"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 "functionalities to L<plackup>, take a look at the L<Plack::Runner> module."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 "=head2 Plack::Middleware"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 "PSGI middleware is a PSGI application that wraps an existing PSGI"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 "application and plays both side of application and servers. From the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 "servers the wrapped code reference still looks like and behaves"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 "exactly the same as PSGI applications."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 "L<Plack::Middleware> gives you an easy way to wrap PSGI applications"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 "with a clean API, and compatibility with L<Plack::Builder> DSL."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 "=head2 Plack::Builder"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 "L<Plack::Builder> gives you a DSL that you can enable Middleware in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 "C<.psgi> files to wrap existent PSGI applications."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 "=head2 Plack::Request, Plack::Response"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 "L<Plack::Request> gives you a nice wrapper API around PSGI C<$env>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 "hash to get headers, cookies and query parameters much like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 "L<Apache::Request> in mod_perl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 "L<Plack::Response> does the same to construct the response array"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 "=head2 Plack::Test"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 "L<Plack::Test> is a unified interface to test your PSGI application"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 "using standard L<HTTP::Request> and L<HTTP::Response> pair with simple"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 "callbacks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "=head2 Plack::Test::Suite"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 "L<Plack::Test::Suite> is a test suite to test a new PSGI server backend."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 "=head1 CONTRIBUTING"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 "=head2 Patches and Bug Fixes"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 "Small patches and bug fixes can be either submitted via nopaste on IRC"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 "L<irc://irc.perl.org/#plack> or L<the github issue"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 "tracker|http://github.com/plack/Plack/issues>.  Forking on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 "L<github|http://github.com/plack/Plack> is another good way if you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 "intend to make larger fixes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "See also L<http://contributing.appspot.com/plack> when you think this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 "document is terribly outdated."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 "=head2 Module Namespaces"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 "Modules added to the Plack:: sub-namespaces should be reasonably generic"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 "components which are useful as building blocks and not just simply using"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 "Plack."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 "Middleware authors are free to use the Plack::Middleware:: namespace for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 "their middleware components. Middleware must be written in the pipeline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 "style such that they can chained together with other middleware components."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "The Plack::Middleware:: modules in the core distribution are good examples"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 "of such modules. It is recommended that you inherit from L<Plack::Middleware>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 "for these types of modules."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 "Not all middleware components are wrappers, but instead are more like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 "endpoints in a middleware chain. These types of components should use the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 "Plack::App:: namespace. Again, look in the core modules to see excellent"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 "examples of these (L<Plack::App::File>, L<Plack::App::Directory>, etc.)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 "It is recommended that you inherit from L<Plack::Component> for these"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 "types of modules."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 "B<DO NOT USE> Plack:: namespace to build a new web application or a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 "framework. It's like naming your application under CGI:: namespace if"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000159 "it's supposed to run on CGI and that is a really bad choice and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000160 "would confuse people badly."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000161 ""
